{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T18:00:51Z","timestamp":1791309651380,"version":"4.3.1"},"reference-count":72,"publisher":"AIP Publishing","issue":"24","funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/100000001","name":"National Science Foundation","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["DMR-1719875"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["DMR-1719875"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100000001","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100001659","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["Emmy Noether"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["Emmy Noether"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100001659","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100001711","name":"Schweizerischer Nationalfonds zur F\u00f6rderung\n                der Wissenschaftlichen Forschung","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["407540 167186 NFP 75 Big Data"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["407540 167186 NFP 75 Big Data"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100001711","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100001711","name":"Schweizerischer Nationalfonds zur F\u00f6rderung\n                der Wissenschaftlichen Forschung","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["PP00P2 138932"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["PP00P2 138932"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100001711","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/100010663","name":"H2020 European Research Council","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["BeStMo"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["BeStMo"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100010663","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":["pubs.aip.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":[],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2018,6,28]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Classical intermolecular potentials typically require an extensive parametrization procedure for any new compound considered. To do away with prior parametrization, we propose a combination of physics-based potentials with machine learning (ML), coined IPML, which is transferable across small neutral organic and biologically relevant molecules. ML models provide on-the-fly predictions for environment-dependent local atomic properties: electrostatic multipole coefficients (significant error reduction compared to previously reported), the population and decay rate of valence atomic densities, and polarizabilities across conformations and chemical compositions of H, C, N, and O atoms. These parameters enable accurate calculations of intermolecular contributions\u2014electrostatics, charge penetration, repulsion, induction\/polarization, and many-body dispersion. Unlike other potentials, this model is transferable in its ability to handle new molecules and conformations without explicit prior parametrization: All local atomic properties are predicted from ML, leaving only eight global parameters\u2014optimized once and for all across compounds. We validate IPML on various gas-phase dimers at and away from equilibrium separation, where we obtain mean absolute errors between 0.4 and 0.7 kcal\/mol for several chemically and conformationally diverse datasets representative of non-covalent interactions in biologically relevant molecules. We further focus on hydrogen-bonded complexes\u2014essential but challenging due to their directional nature\u2014where datasets of DNA base pairs and amino acids yield an extremely encouraging 1.4 kcal\/mol error. Finally, and as a first look, we consider IPML for denser systems: water clusters, supramolecular host-guest complexes, and the benzene crystal.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1063\/1.5009502","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2018,3,15]],"date-time":"2018-03-15T11:34:31Z","timestamp":1521113671000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1063\/aip-crossmark-policy-page","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":176,"title":["Non-covalent interactions across organic and biological subsets of chemical\n          space: Physics-based potentials parametrized from machine learning"],"prefix":"10.1063","volume":"148","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9945-1271","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tristan","family":"Bereau","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Max Planck Institute for Polymer Research 1 , Ackermannweg 10, 55128 Mainz,","place":["Germany"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2732-194X","authenticated-orcid":false,"suffix":"Jr.","given":"Robert A.","family":"DiStasio","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Cornell University 2 , Ithaca, New York 14853,","place":["USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alexandre","family":"Tkatchenko","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Physics and Materials Science Research Unit, University of Luxembourg 3 , L-1511 Luxembourg,","place":["Luxembourg"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"O. Anatole","family":"von Lilienfeld","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Physical Chemistry and National Center for Computational Design and Discovery of Novel Materials (MARVEL), Department of Chemistry, University of Basel 4 , Klingelbergstrasse 80, CH-4056 Basel,","place":["Switzerland"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"317","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2018,3,15]]},"reference":[{"key":"2025100916262982300_c1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"4497","DOI":"10.1021\/ct500573f","volume":"10","year":"2014","journal-title":"J. Chem. Theory Comput."},{"key":"2025100916262982300_c2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"5895","DOI":"10.1021\/acs.jctc.6b00913","volume":"12","year":"2016","journal-title":"J. Chem. Theory Comput."},{"key":"2025100916262982300_c3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"188